1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,808
You get one move. Brake, turn… or do
nothing.

2
00:00:03,808 --> 00:00:07,192
Which decision protects this cargo? Watch
both futures.

3
00:00:07,192 --> 00:00:11,000
Same starting point. One difference: the
command Meta chose.

4
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:16,830
Twinforge tests a decision before
replaying its consequences. Here, one

5
00:00:16,830 --> 00:00:21,043
carrier triggers a chain of collisions.
Without intervention: one hundred sixteen

6
00:00:21,043 --> 00:00:26,021
and a half joules on the protected cargo.
Meta’s steering command brings that

7
00:00:26,021 --> 00:00:31,000
down to four point six. Both futures come
from the same physical model.

8
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:38,667
Muse Spark receives the scene and a menu
of allowed commands. It proposes

9
00:00:38,667 --> 00:00:43,897
one move and explains why. The simulator
calculates the outcome. Those measurements

10
00:00:43,897 --> 00:00:50,000
go back to Spark for revision. The model
never sees the exhaustive search scores.

11
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:55,857
Here are the three proposals that actually
ran. First:

12
00:00:55,857 --> 00:01:00,571
one hundred four point six joules.
Rejected. Second: eight point five.

13
00:01:00,571 --> 00:01:05,286
Still too high. Third: four point six,
under our eight-joule threshold.

14
00:01:05,286 --> 00:01:10,000
You can replay each attempt. The failures
stay in the evidence.

15
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:16,400
This is a recorded real session: three
calls, about thirty-eight seconds,

16
00:01:16,400 --> 00:01:20,400
for less than three US cents. Open the
journal to

17
00:01:20,400 --> 00:01:24,400
inspect the reasoning, then export the
original responses and receipts.

18
00:01:24,400 --> 00:01:28,800
The English text is a translation. The
original evidence remains intact.

19
00:01:28,800 --> 00:01:32,000
Nothing here depends on a convincing
explanation alone.

20
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:39,040
Can a simpler method do better? Here, yes.
The separate local search

21
00:01:39,040 --> 00:01:44,500
tests thirty-seven commands and finds zero
impact on the protected cargo, using less

22
00:01:44,500 --> 00:01:49,960
control effort. That search is not AI. We
show it beside Meta’s result.

23
00:01:49,960 --> 00:01:55,000
The useful thing is a decision you can
challenge, measure, and improve.

24
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:02,542
Now delay the move. At a quarter of a
second, a tested solution exists.

25
00:02:02,542 --> 00:02:07,292
At half a second, none of the thirty-seven
commands passes our criterion.

26
00:02:07,292 --> 00:02:11,250
The collision has already transferred
motion. Twinforge also exposes decisions

27
00:02:11,250 --> 00:02:16,000
that arrive too late. This boundary
belongs to the tested time grid.

28
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:23,532
Change the challenge. At the intersection,
the best nominal command fails when speed

29
00:02:23,532 --> 00:02:29,489
changes by five percent. One successful
check out of three. That is a sensitivity

30
00:02:29,489 --> 00:02:34,170
test, not a safety probability. Inspect
the trajectories. Export the states,

31
00:02:34,170 --> 00:02:38,000
commands, and collisions. Counterexamples
are part of the result.

32
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:44,400
Today, the initial states and physical
laws are supplied. This is

33
00:02:44,400 --> 00:02:49,200
a numerical laboratory, not yet a robot
validated in the real world.

34
00:02:49,200 --> 00:02:53,200
The ambition: test a decision before
facing its consequences. Twinforge.

35
00:02:53,200 --> 00:02:58,000
One decision. Two futures. Now find the
move that makes the difference.
